{
  "availability": "recorded",
  "values": {
    "output": [
      {
        "type": "input_text",
        "text": "Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"
      },
      {
        "type": "input_text",
        "text": "{\"chunk_id\":\"48a00c\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000017183,\"exit_code\":1,\"original_token_count\":5797,\"output\":\"# API — draft\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nHandle login, live messaging, acknowledgments, and storage using the [data contract](../contracts/storage.md).\\nSupport online-only delivery without offline queues or replay.\\n\\n## Behavior\\n\\nApply these rules to client connections and requests.\\n\\n- Exchange JSON messages and replies over one WebSocket connection per client.\\n- Bind successful login to the connection and mark the user online.\\n- Require login for sends and acknowledgments, deriving the acting user from the session.\\n- Remove sessions on connection closure or detected failure, allowing delayed detection of network loss.\\n- Follow the shared [connection behavior](../contracts/websocket.md#connection-behavior) for heartbeat, validation failures, and repeated login.\\n- Validate acknowledgments at the API boundary before passing them to storage.\\n- Log delivery attempts and their outcomes in the server log.\\n- On delivery failure, retain the stored message and take no further action, including retries or sender notifications.\\n\\n## Endpoints\\n\\nUse the endpoint, envelopes, payload types, and correlation rules in the shared [WebSocket contract](../contracts/websocket.md).\\n\\n### Login\\n\\n**Inputs:** [login request](../contracts/websocket.md#login).\\n\\nValidate login before creating a user or binding the connection.\\n\\n- Require `name` to match the [user name format](../contracts/storage.md#users).\\n- Reject a second active login for the same name through the session resolver.\\n\\n**Effects:** Find or create the user and bind the connection.\\n\\n**Outputs:** `login_ok` or `error` returned to the requesting client.\\n\\n### Send\\n\\n**Inputs:** [send request](../contracts/websocket.md#send).\\n\\nValidate the send request before storing the message.\\n\\n- Require an active login and derive `sender_id` from its session.\\n- Require `recipient` to identify an existing, online user.\\n- Require `text` to be present.\\n- Preserve valid `client_sent_at` values and replace missing or malformed values with `0` without rejecting the request.\\n- Replace any client-provided `server_received_at` with the server's receive time.\\n\\n**Effects:** Resolve recipient to user ID, store the message, and attempt delivery once.\\n\\n**Outputs:** `message_accepted` to the sender, `message_received` to the recipient if delivery succeeds, or `error` to the sender on request rejection.\\n\\n### Acknowledge\\n\\n**Inputs:** [ack request](../contracts/websocket.md#ack).\\n\\nValidate the acknowledgment before updating the stored message.\\n\\n- Require an active login.\\n- Require `message_id` to identify an existing message.\\n- Require the message's recipient to match the user bound to the session.\\n- Preserve valid `recipient_received_at` values and replace missing or malformed values with `0` without rejecting the acknowledgment.\\n- Replace any client-provided `server_ack_received_at` with the server's acknowledgment receive time.\\n- Accept repeated acknowledgments while preserving the first recorded timestamps.\\n\\n**Effects:** Record the acknowledgment.\\n\\n**Outputs:** `delivery_receipt` to the sender if connected, or `error` to the acknowledging client on validation failure.\\n\\n## Tables\\n\\n- [users](../contracts/storage.md#users)\\n- [messages](../contracts/storage.md#messages)\\n\\n## Storage\\n\\nApply these operations to in-memory records.\\n\\n- Start with empty `users` and `messages` collections.\\n- Retain records across client disconnections until the server process stops, then discard them.\\n\\n### Find or create user\\n\\n- **Inputs:** Name.\\n- **Effects:** Create with a generated ID only if absent.\\n- **Outputs:** User record.\\n\\n### Find user\\n\\n- **Inputs:** Name.\\n- **Effects:** Look up without creating.\\n- **Outputs:** User record or absent result.\\n\\n### Store message\\n\\n- **Inputs:** Sender and recipient IDs, text, `client_sent_at`, `server_received_at`.\\n- **Effects:** Generate ID and initialize both acknowledgment timestamps to `null`.\\n- **Outputs:** Stored message record.\\n\\n### Record acknowledgment\\n\\n- **Inputs:** Message ID, `recipient_received_at`, `server_ack_received_at`.\\n- **Effects:** Record the first acknowledgment's timestamps.\\n- **Outputs:** Updated message record.\\n\\n## Timestamps\\n\\nApply these rules when receiving events and storing acknowledgments.\\n\\n- Validate client timestamps as UTC timestamps and use `0` for missing or invalid values.\\n- Treat client-reported times as non-authoritative.\\n- Capture server times on event receipt, replacing any client-provided server timestamps.\\n- Preserve event times when writing to storage instead of using storage-write times.\\n- Keep both acknowledgment timestamps null until a validated acknowledgment arrives.\\n- Record both acknowledgment timestamps atomically without changing other message fields.\\n- Preserve the first acknowledgment timestamps on repeated acknowledgments.\\n\\n## Design guide\\n\\nImplement each responsibility below as a separate module with a public interface that hides its implementation and allows behavior to be extended independently.\\n\\n- Encapsulate storage operations for users, messages, and acknowledgments.\\n- Isolate session management for connection bindings, online-user lookup, disconnect cleanup, and duplicate-login policy.\\n- Encapsulate delivery attempts and outcome logging.\\n- Isolate message handling to coordinate sends and acknowledgments through storage, sessions, and delivery.\\n- Isolate the WebSocket API to decode and validate requests, derive the acting user from the session, and format responses.\\n- Keep `User` and `Message` as plain data models.\\n\\n## Constraints\\n\\nApply these constraints to implementation.\\n\\n- Use FastAPI and Uvicorn with one worker process.\\n- Use Pydantic to validate endpoint inputs, preserving the timestamp fallback rules.\\n- Optionally use Pydantic to validate endpoint outputs.\\n- Use uv for dependency management.\\n- Use Ruff for code checks.\\n- Create a Dockerfile for the project.\\n- Use pytest for unit and behavior testing.\\n# Data contract\\n\\n## Users\\n\\n| Field | Type | Meaning |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `id` | UUID | Server-generated, unique user ID. |\\n| `name` | String | Unique name matching `[a-z0-9_]{1,32}`. |\\n\\n## Messages\\n\\n| Field | Type | Meaning |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `id` | UUID | Server-generated, unique message ID. |\\n| `sender_id` | UUID | Reference to the sending user. |\\n| `recipient_id` | UUID | Reference to the receiving user. |\\n| `text` | String | Original message text. |\\n| `client_sent_at` | UTC timestamp or `0` | Sender's local time immediately before sending; `0` means missing or invalid. |\\n| `server_received_at` | UTC timestamp | Server's time when it receives the send request. |\\n| `recipient_received_at` | UTC timestamp, `0`, or `null` | Recipient's local time when it decodes the incoming message, before displaying it; `0` means missing or invalid, and `null` means no acknowledgment recorded. |\\n| `server_ack_received_at` | UTC timestamp or `null` | Server's time when it receives the recipient's acknowledgment. |\\n# WebSocket contract\\n\\nApply this contract to the API and both Python and Rust clients.\\nExchange JSON objects over the WebSocket endpoint `/ws`.\\n\\n## Connection behavior\\n\\nApply these rules to every connection and action.\\n\\n- Have the server send a standard WebSocket ping every 20 seconds on the existing connection.\\n- Have clients respond with pong through their WebSocket library.\\n- Have the server close the connection if the matching pong does not arrive within 20 seconds of the ping.\\n- Have the server return `error` to the requesting client for requests that fail validation, keeping the connection open and existing state unchanged.\\n- Reject another `login` on an already logged-in connection, even for the same name.\\n- Require disconnecting and opening a new connection to switch users.\\n\\n## Envelope\\n\\nUse these fields for requests, replies, and notifications.\\n\\n- `type: string` identifies the action or event listed below.\\n- `correlation_id: UUID` identifies a client request and its direct replies.\\n- `data: object` contains the payload for that action or event.\\n\\nApply these correlation rules to each connection.\\n\\n- Have the client generate a fresh, opaque UUID as `correlation_id` for each request.\\n- Echo the correlation ID in direct replies, including errors.\\n- Omit `correlation_id` from server notifications.\\n- Omit `correlation_id` from an error when the malformed request has no valid correlation ID.\\n- Determine processing and delivery order on the server independently of correlation and message IDs.\\n- Use the server-generated message ID to associate delivery receipts with stored messages.\\n\\nA correlation ID identifies a request, including one rejected before a message exists.\\nA message ID identifies a stored message across delivery and acknowledgment.\\n\\n## Shared types\\n\\nUse these types in the envelope and inside `data`.\\n\\n- `UUID`: JSON string containing a UUID.\\n- `UtcTimestamp`: JSON string containing an ISO 8601 timestamp in UTC.\\n- `ClientTimestampInput`: Any JSON value; preserve a valid `UtcTimestamp` and convert missing or invalid values to the JSON number `0`.\\n\\nUse these wire types for record IDs and timestamps, preserving the `0` and `null` alternatives defined in the [data contract](storage.md).\\n\\n## Client requests\\n\\nUse the action name as `type` and the following fields inside `data`.\\n\\n### login\\n\\n- `name: string`.\\n\\nExample request:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"login\\\",\\n  \\\"correlation_id\\\": \\\"53dd2b06-e8b7-4a19-9c61-9f8df9bf8524\\\",\\n  \\\"data\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"alice\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### send\\n\\n- `recipient: string` — recipient name.\\n- `text: string` — message content.\\n- `client_sent_at: ClientTimestampInput` — optional sender timestamp.\\n\\nExample request:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"send\\\",\\n  \\\"correlation_id\\\": \\\"629a6d45-679d-4e09-8b3a-d6d779ab415c\\\",\\n  \\\"data\\\": {\\n    \\\"recipient\\\": \\\"bob\\\",\\n    \\\"text\\\": \\\"Hi Bob\\\",\\n    \\\"client_sent_at\\\": \\\"2026-11-08T12:00:00Z\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIf Bob is offline, the server correlates the error with that request.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"error\\\",\\n  \\\"correlation_id\\\": \\\"629a6d45-679d-4e09-8b3a-d6d779ab415c\\\",\\n  \\\"data\\\": {\\n    \\\"message\\\": \\\"Recipient is offline\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### ack\\n\\n- `message_id: UUID` — message being acknowledged.\\n- `recipient_received_at: ClientTimestampInput` — optional recipient timestamp.\\n\\nExample request acknowledging a received message:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"ack\\\",\\n  \\\"correlation_id\\\": \\\"b4a0fa95-9c1c-472e-98ae-42fdc01c8aa7\\\",\\n  \\\"data\\\": {\\n    \\\"message_id\\\": \\\"6ab59054-8e3c-4b20-9a11-32922d384f68\\\",\\n    \\\"recipient_received_at\\\": \\\"2026-11-08T12:01:00Z\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Server events\\n\\nUse these event names and payloads for replies and notifications.\\n\\n### login_ok\\n\\nReply to a successful login with `data` matching the [users contract](storage.md#users).\\n\\n### message_accepted\\n\\nReply to an accepted send with `data` matching the [messages contract](storage.md#messages).\\nAcceptance confirms storage only; delivery remains unconfirmed without a `delivery_receipt`.\\n\\n### message_received\\n\\nNotify the recipient of an incoming message with `data` matching the [messages contract](storage.md#messages).\\n\\n### delivery_receipt\\n\\nNotify the original sender of a valid acknowledgment if still connected, using these fields inside `data`.\\n\\n- `message_id: UUID`.\\n- `recipient_received_at: UtcTimestamp | 0`.\\n- `server_ack_received_at: UtcTimestamp`.\\n\\nA valid `ack` produces a delivery receipt for the original sender without a separate success reply to the acknowledging client.\\n\\n### error\\n\\nReply to the client whose request failed validation or was rejected, including an invalid `ack`, using this field inside `data`.\\n\\n- `message: string`.\\n# Execution records\\n\\n## Allocate or resume\\n\\n- Context: apply the [record guidelines](#record-guidelines) when saving, handing off or resuming work.\\n- Inputs:\\n  - New work: current brief or bundle and allowed memory.\\n  - Resumed work: identified record paths.\\n- Effects:\\n  - For new work, run `python3 agentic-workflow/helpers/records.py allocate NAME` before starting tasks.\\n  - Look up resumed task IDs only in that implementation's immediate `tasks/` directory.\\n  - Ask when the implementation, task or resume scope is missing or unclear.\\n  - For resumed work, run `python3 agentic-workflow/helpers/records.py resolve IMPLEMENTATION TASK`.\\n  - Use complete records, findings and current files for that work.\\n  - Recheck outdated or missing evidence.\\n- Outputs: implementation root and task ID.\\n- Next: reuse the root for resumed work and repairs.\\n\\n`allocate` reserves `.agentic-workflow/records/<NNN>_<name>/` under a local directory lock.\\nIt attempts to capture session metadata and the current native turn.\\n`resolve` finds the task and attempts the same capture.\\nCodex supplies its thread identity directly; OpenCode 2.2.6 supplies\\n[native shell identity](../guides/TRACING.md#enable-opencode-capture).\\nCapture failures appear on stderr without blocking allocation or resolution.\\n\\n## Recover originals\\n\\n- Context: use when approved original documents are missing.\\n- Inputs: known task sources or conversations, and the missing document paths.\\n- Effects:\\n  - Recover originals from known sources.\\n  - Do not recreate originals from summaries.\\n  - Ask for unavailable originals with a list of checked locations.\\n  - Give independent workers recovered sources or acceptance criteria without recovery conversations.\\n  - In Plan mode, present full drafts without saving.\\n  - Once editing is available, save approved text before implementation or handoff.\\n- Outputs: recovered approved originals or a report of what is still missing.\\n\\n## Hand off work\\n\\n- Context: apply [Checks](CHECKS.md) and [Memory](MEMORY.md) when choosing what a recipient may read.\\n- Inputs:\\n  - Implementation root, task ID and mode.\\n  - Entry points, role instructions and guideline paths.\\n  - Allowed sources, work to check and evidence.\\n  - Accepted contracts, agreed interfaces and file owners.\\n  - Prior findings and an output path.\\n- Effects:\\n  - Pass absolute paths or paths relative to a stated root.\\n  - Pass paths rather than document copies.\\n  - Include plans and memory only when the recipient's role allows them.\\n  - Search only for inputs still missing.\\n  - Keep worker handles and output paths for return handoffs.\\n  - Send implementation corrections through [Supervisor](../roles/SUPERVISOR.md).\\n  - Send document corrections to their author.\\n  - Give an unavailable owner's replacement only allowed saved inputs.\\n  - Report owner replacements.\\n  - Include the assignment line below.\\n  - Have recipients repeat the assignment line in their own commentary.\\n- Outputs: allowed input paths, recipient handle and assignment line.\\n\\n## Record guidelines\\n\\nApply to task documents, handoffs and results.\\n\\n- Read files at their exact paths, even when someone else supplied the paths.\\n- Start new work from current authorized inputs without searching earlier tasks or conversations.\\n- Stop searching once the sources that govern the work are known.\\n- Store records in the locations below.\\n- Use source filenames without extensions as task IDs.\\n- Reserve `orchestration` for Supervisor activity and `validation` for final validation.\\n- Follow the assigned [Implementer mode](../roles/IMPLEMENTER.md) to decide which documents are required.\\n- Use `plan.md` and `acceptance.md` for required task documents.\\n- Follow the [plan](../templates/plan.md), [acceptance](../templates/acceptance.md) and [result](../templates/result.md) templates.\\n- Preserve approved originals and their history in place.\\n- Save one short result per role and run.\\n- Link extra evidence and usage reports.\\n- Keep failure and repair history.\\n- Avoid routine manifests, checksum lists, duplicate summaries and repeated hash scans.\\n- Keep useful hashes for identity, deduplication or regression checks.\\n- Keep native logs local.\\n- Give independent workers usage measurements and metadata in JSON or Markdown.\\n- Exclude `events.json`, `timeline.json` and dashboard evidence from independent inputs because they contain conversations by default.\\n- Report missing telemetry as a reporting limit.\\n- Do not block app acceptance for missing telemetry.\\n- Leave unknown values unavailable.\\n- Apply [Tracing](../guides/TRACING.md) for capture throughout execution, usage reports and assignment recovery.\\n\\n| Record | Location under the implementation root |\\n|---|---|\\n| Task IDs and documents | `tasks/<task-id>/` |\\n| Results and evidence | `runs/<task-id>/<agent-run-id>/` |\\n| Native turns | `native/<session-id>/<turn-id>.jsonl` (Codex) or `.json` (OpenCode) |\\n| Reports | `reports/` |\\n\\n## Assignment format\\n\\nApply to the assignment line sent during handoff.\\n\\n- Fill in the implementation name, source task ID and recipient role.\\n- Put the assignment marker at the start of its own line.\\n- Use the optional `purpose` field to describe the assignment.\\n- Keep the assignment for follow-ups unless explicitly retagged.\\n- Keep replacement workers' native identities separate.\\n- Use `supervisor` / `orchestration` for Supervisor turns and `validator` / `validation` for final validation.\\n- Emit your own assignment line in commentary after allocation or resume.\\n\\n```text\\nAGENTIC_WORKFLOW {\\\"implementation\\\":\\\"001_todolist\\\",\\\"task\\\":\\\"01_business_logic\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"implementer\\\"}\\n```\\n# Checks and acceptance\\n\\nRead when preparing checks, reviewing work or deciding whether it passes.\\n\\n- Resolve requirements through [Bundle](BUNDLE.md).\\n- Apply engineering rules from [Guidelines](GUIDELINES.md).\\n\\n## Prepare acceptance\\n\\nApply before coding in plan-review mode and when authoring or updating requested acceptance documents.\\n\\n- Derive acceptance criteria from original requirements, excluding advisory implementation suggestions.\\n- Cover normal use, boundaries, failures, interactions and specified state or lifecycle guarantees.\\n- State component contracts in acceptance or link their authoritative definitions.\\n- Include inputs, outputs, errors and observable side effects in each contract.\\n- Link contracts to source requirements.\\n- Map task acceptance outcomes and contract guarantees to concrete check scenarios.\\n- Link shared scenarios and add only missing task-specific checks.\\n- State setup, action and expected outcome for each scenario.\\n- Choose unit or integration tests for each scenario.\\n- Use both test layers where they cover distinct risks.\\n- Keep internal design choices in the plan unless the requirements specify them.\\n- Correct incomplete acceptance criteria against the original requirements.\\n- Preserve approved history during acceptance corrections.\\n- Rerun checks affected by acceptance corrections.\\n- Repeat required independent review after acceptance corrections.\\n\\n## Prepare checks\\n\\nApply before coding and when updating checks in either mode.\\n\\n- Use source criteria directly for direct tasks without writing separate acceptance.\\n- Treat a missing requirement as a defect, even if all listed checks pass.\\n- Choose checks that fit the change and test observable behaviour.\\n- Include relevant regression tests.\\n- Run required manual checks.\\n- Run required checks against real dependencies.\\n- Cover test gaps through public interfaces.\\n- Record why mocks fit, what they replace and what remains untested in check evidence.\\n- Assess internal tests and mocks by the failures they can detect.\\n- Consider isolation, cost and realism.\\n- Require meaningful assertions about the code under test.\\n- Reject production hooks added only for tests.\\n\\n## Assess results\\n\\nApply when reporting checks or accepting work.\\n\\n- Use `PASS` only with passing required checks, adequate requirement coverage and no actionable findings.\\n- Check coverage separately from test success.\\n- Use `FAIL` when the work breaks a requirement or an applicable rule.\\n- Use `BLOCKED` for unavailable required tools or capabilities, or unresolved requirements.\\n- Mark skipped checks `NOT_RUN`, or `BLOCKED` when their absence prevents acceptance.\\n- Block acceptance with `INDEPENDENT_REVIEW_NOT_RUN` when a required fresh review has not run.\\n- Report substitute checks and coverage gaps without lowering the requirements.\\n- Save evidence using the [result template](../templates/result.md).\\n- For manual judgments, name the rule and the item inspected.\\n- Report cleanup errors separately from the failure being checked.\\n\\n## Independent checks\\n\\nApply to independent review and validation.\\n\\n- Start with a fresh context and no inherited conversation.\\n- Choose checks from original requirements and guidelines before reading acceptance or code.\\n- Limit inputs to requirements, applicable acceptance, candidate work, factual results, contracts, justified guideline exceptions and prior findings.\\n- Allow focused mock justifications as review inputs.\\n- Assess mock justifications independently.\\n- Exclude plans, implementation suggestions, conversations, project memory, design explanations and claims of correctness.\\n- Exclude copies of these from other files too.\\n- Keep the work being checked unchanged, including relevant dependencies, configuration and check inputs.\\n- Record its location and revision when available.\\n- Use existing version information.\\n- Do not require a new commit or lock.\\n- Report candidate changes during checking.\\n- Rerun affected independent checks in a fresh context.\\n- Run relevant checks yourself.\\n- Reproduce reported problems where feasible.\\n- Follow the stated setup, cleanup, pass criteria, sampling rules and thresholds.\\n\\n## Keep results current\\n\\nApply while running checks and when stopping work.\\n\\n- Batch reads and commands that do not depend on each other.\\n- Repeat successful checks only for relevant changes, failures, new concerns or required independent checks.\\n- Reassess affected results when requirements, guidelines, dependencies or implementation change.\\n- Save unfinished work and blockers when cancelled or stopped by a limit.\\n# Document style\\n\\nApply in every role only when writing or editing project documents, including workflow documentation.\\nSource code, code comments and docstrings are outside this policy's scope.\\n\\n- Describe workflows in this order: Context → Inputs → Effects → Outputs → Next.\\n- Omit Next when unnecessary.\\n- Format guideline sections like [Engineering guidelines](GUIDELINES.md): short title, applicability sentence and flat imperative bullets.\\n- Write one short, single-sentence rule per list item.\\n- Keep related details together when the rule remains short.\\n- Use bullets for rules and numbered lists only for action sequences.\\n- Separate procedural sections from guideline sections.\\n- Link to each shared policy rule's authoritative home instead of repeating it.\\n\\n## Workflow contract\\n\\nApply when changing workflow behavior or its documentation.\\n\\n- Treat [WORKFLOW.md](../WORKFLOW.md) as the authoritative source of workflow behavior.\\n- Update it whenever workflow behavior changes.\\n- Express changes as brief statements of core responsibilities and outcomes.\\n- Keep procedures, state formats and implementation details in role documents or policies.\\n- Derive complete execution duties in role documents from it.\\n- Keep those documents free of references back to the contract.\\n- Keep derived instructions consistent with the workflow contract.\\n.\\n..\\n.agentic-workflow\\n.agents\\n.aws\\n.codex\\n.git\\n.gitignore\\nAGENTS.md\\nMakefile\\nagentic-workflow\\nnotes\\nspecs\\ncat: .agentic-workflow/config/MODELS.md: No such file or directory\\n\"}"
