        "type": "input_text",
        "text": "{\"chunk_id\":\"d80c93\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000010069,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":5761,\"output\":\"<!-- agentic-workflow:start -->\\n# Agentic Workflow\\n\\n- Before planning, changing or reviewing code, tests or tooling, read\\n  [Engineering guidelines](agentic-workflow/policies/GUIDELINES.md).\\n- For specification implementation, read\\n  [Workflow entry points](agentic-workflow/AGENTS.md) and act as Supervisor.\\n- For document authoring or workflow maintenance, read those entry points\\n  and act as Planner.\\n- Before authoring or editing project documents,\\n  read [Document style](agentic-workflow/policies/DOCUMENT_STYLE.md), regardless of role.\\n- Ordinary planning and implementation follows\\n  [Project memory](agentic-workflow/policies/MEMORY.md); implement ordinary work directly.\\n- Keep project inputs, application code, memory and execution evidence\\n  outside `agentic-workflow/`.\\n<!-- agentic-workflow:end -->\\n\\nFor ordinary work, apply the guidelines, run relevant checks, inspect the diff and\\nreport results. Formal workflow artifacts and roles are optional unless requested.\\nPlan-only requests stop at the plan; preserve explicit approval requirements.\\nWorkflow helpers may be changed when workflow/tooling implementation is requested.\\nAdvice/tooling work uses requested files and relevant references without task-history\\ndiscovery. For the repository's todolist eval, read [the eval guide](evals/README.md).\\n# Implementer\\n\\n## Execute a task\\n\\n- Context:\\n  - Read [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md), [Bundle](../policies/BUNDLE.md), [Checks](../policies/CHECKS.md) and [Records](../policies/RECORDS.md).\\n  - Ordinary work also uses [Memory](../policies/MEMORY.md).\\n- Inputs: assignment, sources, candidate, accepted contracts and mode.\\n- Effects:\\n  - Read inputs.\\n  - Agree on scope and file ownership with Supervisor before concurrent edits.\\n  - Follow the assigned mode below.\\n  - Check requirements and regressions.\\n  - Inspect the diff.\\n  - Record check evidence.\\n  - Include mock justifications under Checks.\\n  - Fix defects.\\n  - Rerun affected checks.\\n  - Preserve approved originals when fixing source omissions.\\n  - Plan-review: complete review below.\\n  - Save the [result](../templates/result.md), mode, evidence and contracts.\\n  - Include applicable memory/usage paths.\\n  - Report blockers promptly.\\n  - Report completion once with the result path.\\n- Outputs: candidate, evidence, task result and required documents.\\n- Next:\\n  - Return result to Supervisor for acceptance.\\n  - Keep this context for corrections.\\n  - Repeat affected task steps.\\n\\n## Plan-review mode\\n\\nApply when assigned `plan-review` mode.\\n\\n- Write [plan.md](../templates/plan.md) before code.\\n- Write [acceptance.md](../templates/acceptance.md) before code.\\n- Link acceptance to sources.\\n- Include public API contracts under Checks.\\n- Include expected test scenarios under Checks.\\n- Implement the task.\\n\\n## Direct mode\\n\\nApply when assigned `direct` mode.\\n\\n- Implement against source criteria.\\n- Run checks against source criteria.\\n- Skip plan, separate acceptance and task review.\\n- Keep the assigned mode.\\n\\n## Review a plan-review task\\n\\n- Context: apply [Checks](../policies/CHECKS.md) and [Reviewer](REVIEWER.md).\\n- Inputs: checked candidate, requirements, acceptance, factual evidence and agreed review boundary.\\n- Effects:\\n  - Agree on review boundary with Supervisor.\\n  - Wait for confirmation that affected writers are paused.\\n  - Spawn fresh Reviewer with permitted inputs.\\n  - Pass mock justifications with check evidence.\\n  - Report review completion to Supervisor.\\n  - Wait for explicit release before writing.\\n  - Fix actionable findings.\\n  - Rerun affected checks.\\n  - Repeat with fresh Reviewer.\\n  - Keep task ownership through corrections.\\n- Outputs: review result, findings, corrections, recheck evidence or blockers.\\n- Next:\\n  - After review passes, return task result to Supervisor.\\n# Engineering guidelines\\n\\nRead before planning, production code, tests, tooling, review or repairs.\\n\\n- Separate state and logic.\\n- Use classes that mix state and logic only when required.\\n- Localize side effects.\\n- Keep each module's top-level flow clear and functional.\\n- Prefer small, Unix-like modules.\\n- Give each module a public API with few responsibilities.\\n- Prioritize public behaviour tests.\\n- Internal tests can add useful coverage.\\n- Prefer real infrastructure.\\n- Justify mocks under [Checks](CHECKS.md).\\n- Compose larger structures from small modules.\\n- Test public behaviour for every eligible module.\\n- Test public contracts specified in the task.\\n- Add validations, locks, checksums and manifests only when needed or explicitly requested.\\n- Do not create backwards-compatible functionality unless explicitly requested.\\n- Use simple language in comments and documents.\\n- Avoid dense jargon and stock AI phrasing.\\n# Model routing\\n\\nRead on role entry and before launching workers.\\n\\n## Routing source\\n\\n- Context: choose routing on role entry and before launching workers.\\n- Inputs: assigned workspace and optional `<project-root>/.agentic-workflow/config/MODELS.md`.\\n- Effects:\\n  1. Find the project root from the assigned workspace.\\n  2. Use the project file if present, otherwise this file.\\n  3. Correct missing or invalid project routes without falling back to this file.\\n- Outputs: chosen policy path.\\n- Next:\\n  - Pass the chosen policy path in handoffs.\\n  - Reread the chosen policy before launching more workers.\\n\\nThe project file replaces this entire policy, including all tables.\\nThe eval launcher uses the same selection rule for the Supervisor's default route.\\n\\nTo customize routing, copy this file to the project path and edit it there.\\n\\n## Select settings\\n\\n- Context: read the latest chosen policy and its tables before launching workers.\\n- Inputs: role, task conditions, profiles and role routes.\\n- Effects:\\n  1. Check that routes are valid and refer to defined profiles.\\n  2. Correct errors before choosing a route.\\n  3. Select the first matching route below.\\n  4. Request its settings through controls the host supports.\\n- Outputs:\\n  - Record the profile and reason in the role's result.\\n  - Record requested and observed settings separately.\\n  - Leave unknown observations unavailable.\\n\\n## Routing guidelines\\n\\nApply when requesting or changing worker settings.\\n\\n- Use host defaults for unsupported settings.\\n- Report fallbacks.\\n- Change existing worker settings only through supported controls.\\n- Preserve worker context and fresh-review requirements when changing settings.\\n- Keep current settings when the session cannot change them.\\n- Without session controls, use top-level routing only to guide session launches.\\n- Report known differences between requested and actual settings.\\n- Keep model names and supported reasoning values in this policy.\\n- Use exact model identifiers, including provider prefixes.\\n- Use `default` to keep the host setting.\\n- Keep authentication and connections in the host configuration.\\n- Do not add a dispatcher, adapters or a Markdown parser to apply these tables.\\n\\n## Route selection\\n\\n| First matching condition | Route |\\n|---|---|\\n| Two consecutive implementation repairs reproduce the same failure without new diagnostic evidence | `escalation` |\\n| Conflicting requirements, complex interactions, or correctness decisions involving security, data loss or compatibility | Role's demanding route |\\n| Local implementation, explicit behavior, established pattern, no interface or dependency changes | `small-change` |\\n| Otherwise | Role default |\\n\\n## Profiles\\n\\n| Profile | Model | Reasoning |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| coordination | `gpt-6.1-sol` | `low` |\\n| standard | `gpt-6.1-sol` | `medium` |\\n| careful | `gpt-6.1-sol` | `high` |\\n| small-change | `gpt-6-luna` | `high` |\\n| escalation | `gpt-6-astra` | `medium` |\\n\\n## Role routes\\n\\n| Role | Default | Demanding work |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Supervisor | coordination | standard |\\n| Planner | standard | careful |\\n| Implementer | standard | careful |\\n| Reviewer | careful | escalation |\\n| Validator | careful | escalation |\\n# Checks and acceptance\\n\\nRead when preparing checks, reviewing work or deciding whether it passes.\\n\\n- Resolve requirements through [Bundle](BUNDLE.md).\\n- Apply engineering rules from [Guidelines](GUIDELINES.md).\\n\\n## Prepare acceptance\\n\\nApply before coding in plan-review mode and when authoring or updating requested acceptance documents.\\n\\n- Derive acceptance criteria from original requirements, excluding advisory implementation suggestions.\\n- Cover normal use, boundaries, failures, interactions and specified state or lifecycle guarantees.\\n- State component contracts in acceptance or link their authoritative definitions.\\n- Include inputs, outputs, errors and observable side effects in each contract.\\n- Link contracts to source requirements.\\n- Map task acceptance outcomes and contract guarantees to concrete check scenarios.\\n- Link shared scenarios and add only missing task-specific checks.\\n- State setup, action and expected outcome for each scenario.\\n- Choose unit or integration tests for each scenario.\\n- Use both test layers where they cover distinct risks.\\n- Keep internal design choices in the plan unless the requirements specify them.\\n- Correct incomplete acceptance criteria against the original requirements.\\n- Preserve approved history during acceptance corrections.\\n- Rerun checks affected by acceptance corrections.\\n- Repeat required independent review after acceptance corrections.\\n\\n## Prepare checks\\n\\nApply before coding and when updating checks in either mode.\\n\\n- Use source criteria directly for direct tasks without writing separate acceptance.\\n- Treat a missing requirement as a defect, even if all listed checks pass.\\n- Choose checks that fit the change and test observable behaviour.\\n- Include relevant regression tests.\\n- Run required manual checks.\\n- Run required checks against real dependencies.\\n- Cover test gaps through public interfaces.\\n- Record why mocks fit, what they replace and what remains untested in check evidence.\\n- Assess internal tests and mocks by the failures they can detect.\\n- Consider isolation, cost and realism.\\n- Require meaningful assertions about the code under test.\\n- Reject production hooks added only for tests.\\n\\n## Assess results\\n\\nApply when reporting checks or accepting work.\\n\\n- Use `PASS` only with passing required checks, adequate requirement coverage and no actionable findings.\\n- Check coverage separately from test success.\\n- Use `FAIL` when the work breaks a requirement or an applicable rule.\\n- Use `BLOCKED` for unavailable required tools or capabilities, or unresolved requirements.\\n- Mark skipped checks `NOT_RUN`, or `BLOCKED` when their absence prevents acceptance.\\n- Block acceptance with `INDEPENDENT_REVIEW_NOT_RUN` when a required fresh review has not run.\\n- Report substitute checks and coverage gaps without lowering the requirements.\\n- Save evidence using the [result template](../templates/result.md).\\n- For manual judgments, name the rule and the item inspected.\\n- Report cleanup errors separately from the failure being checked.\\n\\n## Independent checks\\n\\nApply to independent review and validation.\\n\\n- Start with a fresh context and no inherited conversation.\\n- Choose checks from original requirements and guidelines before reading acceptance or code.\\n- Limit inputs to requirements, applicable acceptance, candidate work, factual results, contracts, justified guideline exceptions and prior findings.\\n- Allow focused mock justifications as review inputs.\\n- Assess mock justifications independently.\\n- Exclude plans, implementation suggestions, conversations, project memory, design explanations and claims of correctness.\\n- Exclude copies of these from other files too.\\n- Keep the work being checked unchanged, including relevant dependencies, configuration and check inputs.\\n- Record its location and revision when available.\\n- Use existing version information.\\n- Do not require a new commit or lock.\\n- Report candidate changes during checking.\\n- Rerun affected independent checks in a fresh context.\\n- Run relevant checks yourself.\\n- Reproduce reported problems where feasible.\\n- Follow the stated setup, cleanup, pass criteria, sampling rules and thresholds.\\n\\n## Keep results current\\n\\nApply while running checks and when stopping work.\\n\\n- Batch reads and commands that do not depend on each other.\\n- Repeat successful checks only for relevant changes, failures, new concerns or required independent checks.\\n- Reassess affected results when requirements, guidelines, dependencies or implementation change.\\n- Save unfinished work and blockers when cancelled or stopped by a limit.\\n# Execution records\\n\\n## Allocate or resume\\n\\n- Context: apply the [record guidelines](#record-guidelines) when saving, handing off or resuming work.\\n- Inputs:\\n  - New work: current brief or bundle and allowed memory.\\n  - Resumed work: identified record paths.\\n- Effects:\\n  - For new work, run `python3 agentic-workflow/helpers/records.py allocate NAME` before starting tasks.\\n  - Look up resumed task IDs only in that implementation's immediate `tasks/` directory.\\n  - Ask when the implementation, task or resume scope is missing or unclear.\\n  - For resumed work, run `python3 agentic-workflow/helpers/records.py resolve IMPLEMENTATION TASK`.\\n  - Use complete records, findings and current files for that work.\\n  - Recheck outdated or missing evidence.\\n- Outputs: implementation root and task ID.\\n- Next: reuse the root for resumed work and repairs.\\n\\n`allocate` reserves `.agentic-workflow/records/<NNN>_<name>/` under a local directory lock.\\nIt attempts to capture session metadata and the current native turn.\\n`resolve` finds the task and attempts the same capture.\\nCodex supplies its thread identity directly; OpenCode 2.2.6 supplies\\n[native shell identity](../guides/TRACING.md#enable-opencode-capture).\\nCapture failures appear on stderr without blocking allocation or resolution.\\n\\n## Recover originals\\n\\n- Context: use when approved original documents are missing.\\n- Inputs: known task sources or conversations, and the missing document paths.\\n- Effects:\\n  - Recover originals from known sources.\\n  - Do not recreate originals from summaries.\\n  - Ask for unavailable originals with a list of checked locations.\\n  - Give independent workers recovered sources or acceptance criteria without recovery conversations.\\n  - In Plan mode, present full drafts without saving.\\n  - Once editing is available, save approved text before implementation or handoff.\\n- Outputs: recovered approved originals or a report of what is still missing.\\n\\n## Hand off work\\n\\n- Context: apply [Checks](CHECKS.md) and [Memory](MEMORY.md) when choosing what a recipient may read.\\n- Inputs:\\n  - Implementation root, task ID and mode.\\n  - Entry points, role instructions and guideline paths.\\n  - Allowed sources, work to check and evidence.\\n  - Accepted contracts, agreed interfaces and file owners.\\n  - Prior findings and an output path.\\n- Effects:\\n  - Pass absolute paths or paths relative to a stated root.\\n  - Pass paths rather than document copies.\\n  - Include plans and memory only when the recipient's role allows them.\\n  - Search only for inputs still missing.\\n  - Keep worker handles and output paths for return handoffs.\\n  - Send implementation corrections through [Supervisor](../roles/SUPERVISOR.md).\\n  - Send document corrections to their author.\\